¶
{{smaller block|Unionism hitherto has been presented to the ''unskilled'' in far too costly and elaborate a form.
{{right|''Nineteenth Century'', XXVI. 728.}}}}

'''2.''' Destitute of practical knowledge; unacquainted; unfamiliar: as, ''unskilled'' in chemistry.—'''3.''' Produced without skill or dexterity; showing no evidence of skill in production.

{{smaller block|If their ''unskilled'' verses were preserved at all, they must have been preserved by those who repeated them from memory. {{right|''G. Ticknor'', Span. Lit., I. 107.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Unskilled labor,''' labor that does not exhibit or does not require special skill or training: usually confined to the simpler forms of manual labor, as the labor of hod-carriers, etc.}}

{{smaller block|''Unskilled labor'', requiring only brawny muscle, cannot equitably claim the wages of skilled labor, which taxes the brain, and requires the drill of a long apprenticeship.
{{right|''R. D. Hitchcock'', Add. on the 48th Anniversary, Union [Theol. Seminary.}}}}

'''unslain''' (un-slān′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unslaine'', ''unslagen''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''slain''<sup>1</sup>.] Not slain. ''Wars of Alexander'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2475.

'''unslaked''' (un-slākt′), ''a.'' [< ME. *''unslaked'', also ''unslekked''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''slaked'', pp. of ''slake''<sup>1</sup>.] Not slaked, in any sense.

{{smaller block|''Unslekked'' lym, chalk, and gleyre of an ey.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Canon’s Yeoman’s Tale, l. 253.}}}}

'''unsleeping''' (un-slē′ping), ''a.'' Not sleeping; ever wakeful.

{{smaller block|The ''unsleeping'' eyes of God. {{right|''Milton'', P. L., v. 647.}}}}

'''unslept†''' (un-slept′), ''a.'' Having been without sleep.

{{smaller block|Pale as man longe ''unslept''. {{right|''The Isle of Ladies'', l. 1836.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Tip-toe she tripped it o’er the floor;
She drew the bar, ''unsnecked'' the door.</poem>
{{right|''Jamieson’s Popular Ballads.''}}}}

'''unsoaped''' (un-sōpt′), ''a.'' Not soaped; unwashed. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The ''unsoaped'' of Ipswich brought up the rear.
{{right|''Dickens'', Pickwick, xxiv.}}}}
{{smaller block|There was a wild-haired ''unsoaped'' boy.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Old Vol. of Life, p. 59.}}}}

'''unsociability''' (un-sō-shia̤-bil′i-ti), ''n.'' The state of being unsociable; unsociableness.

'''unsociable''' (un-sō′shia̤-bl), ''a.'' Not sociable, in any sense.
{{smaller block|Whom, when Time hath made ''unsociable'' to others, we become a burden to ourselves.
{{right|''Raleigh'' (Arber’s Eng. Garner, I. 139).}}}}
{{smaller block|Such a behaviour deters men from a religious life, by representing it as an ''unsociable'' state, that extinguishes all joy. {{right|''Addison.''}}}}

'''unsociableness''' (un-sō′shia̤-bl-nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being unsociable; unsociability.

'''unsociably''' (un-sō′shia̤-bli), ''adv.'' In an unsocial manner; with reserve. ''Sir R. L’Estrange.''

'''unsocial''' (un-sō′shal), ''a.'' Not social; not adapted to society; not tending to sociability; reserved; unsociable. ''Shenstone.''

'''unsocialism''' (un-sō′shal-izm), ''n.'' [< ''unsocial'' + ''-ism''.] The state of being unsocial; reserve; unsociability. ''Congregationalist'', Jan. 27, 1887. [Rare.]

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''unsociality''' (un-sō-shi-al′i̤-ti), ''n.'' The state of being unsocial; unsociability. ''W. Hazlitt'', in Personal Traits of Brit. Authors (Wordsworth), p. 181.

'''unsocket''' (un-sok′et), ''v. t.'' To take from a socket.

'''unsoft†''' (un-sôft′), ''a.'' [ME. ''unsofte'', < AS. ''unsōfte'', hard, severe, < ''un-'', not, + ''sōfte'', soft, mild: see ''un-''<sup>1</sup> and ''soft''.] Hard; harsh.
{{smaller block|Thilke brustles of his berd ''unsofte''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Merchant’s Tale, l. 580.}}}}

'''unsoft†''' (un-sôft′), ''adv.'' Not with softness; not softly. ''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., July.

'''unsolder''' (un-sod′ėr), ''v. t.'' To separate, as what is joined by solder; disunite; dissolve; break up. ''Tennyson'', Passing of Arthur.

'''unsoldiered†''' (un-sōl′jėrd), ''a.'' Not having the qualities of a soldier; not having the qualifications or appearance of trained soldiers. ''Fletcher'', Loyal Subject, i. 2.

'''unsolemn''' (un-sol′em), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unsolempne''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''solemn''.] Not solemn. {{smaller|(''a'') Not sacred, serious, or grave. (''b'') Not accompanied by the due ceremonies or forms; not regular or formal; legally informal: as, an ''unsolemn'' testament. ''Aylife'', Parergon, p. 525. (''c''†) Uncelebrated; unknown to fame.}}
{{smaller block|The renon nis neyther over-old ne ''unsolempne''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, i. prose 3.}}}}

'''unsolemnize''' (un-sol′em-nīz), ''v. t.'' [< ''unsolemn'' + ''-ize''.] To divest of solemnity; render unsolemn.

'''unsolicited''' (un-sō-lis′i-ted), ''a.'' Not solicited. {{smaller|(''a'') Not applied to or petitioned.}}
{{smaller block|Not a god left ''unsolicited''. {{right|''Shak.'', Tit. And., iv. 3. 60.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
(''b'') Not asked for; not requested: as, ''unsolicited'' interference. ''Lord Halifax.''}}

'''unsolicitous''' (un-sọ̄-lis′i-tus), ''a.'' Not solicitous. {{smaller|(''a'') Not deeply concerned or anxious. ''A. Tucker.'' (''b'') Not marked or occupied by care, anxiety, or solicitude: as, ''unsolicitous'' hours. ''Johnson.''}}

'''unsolid''' (un-sol′id), ''a.'' Not solid. {{smaller|(''a'') Not having the properties of a solid; liquid or gaseous. ''Locke'', Human Understanding, ii. 4. (''b'') Not sound, substantial, or firm; empty; weak; vain; ill-founded.}}

'''unsolidity''' (un-sọ̄-lid′i-ti), ''n.'' The character or state of being unsolid, in any sense. ''The Atlantic'', LXIII. 655.

'''unsolved''' (un-solvd′), ''a.'' Not solved, explained, or cleared up: as, an ''unsolved'' riddle. ''Dryden'', Virgil, Ded.

'''unsonsy, unsoncy''' (un-son′si), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not sonsy; not buxom, plump, or good-looking. [Scotch.]—'''2.''' Bringing or boding ill luck; unlucky; ill-omened; unpropitious. [Prov. Eng. and Scotch.]

Also spelled ''unsonsie, unsoncie.''

'''unsoot†''', ''a.'' An obsolete variant of ''unsweet.''
{{smaller block|And cast hem out as rotten and ''unsoote.''
{{right|''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., December.}}}}

'''unsophisticate''' (un-sọ̄-fis′ti-kāt), ''a.'' Unsophisticated.
{{smaller block|<poem>Nature, ''unsophisticate'' by man,
Starts not aside from her Creator’s plan.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', Conversation, l. 451.}}}}

'''unsophisticated''' (un-sọ̄-fis′ti-kā-ted), ''a.'' Not sophisticated; not corrupted, adulterated, or perverted by art; unmixed; pure; genuine; not artificial; simple; artless.

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|It is the only place in England where these stuffs are made ''unsophisticated.''
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, July 8, 1656.}}
Sidney had the good sense to feel that it was ''unsophisticated'' sentiment rather than rusticity of phrase that befitted such themes.
{{right|''Lowell'', Among my Books, 2d ser., p. 135.}}}}

'''unsophisticatedness''' (un-sō-fis′ti-kā-ted-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being unsophisticated; genuineness; artlessness.

'''unsophistication''' (un-sọ̄-fis-ti-kā′shọn), ''n.'' Simplicity; artlessness; unsophisticatedness.

'''unsorrowed''' (un-sor′ōd), ''a.'' Not sorrowed, grieved, or mourned (for); not lamented or regretted: sometimes followed by ''for.''
{{smaller block|Transgressions . . . ''unsorrowed for'' and repented of.
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, v. 72.}}
Die, like a fool, ''unsorrowed.''
{{right|''Fletcher'', Monsieur Thomas.}}}}

'''unsorted''' (un-sôr′ted), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not sorted; not arranged or put in order; not assorted or classified. ''Watts'', On the Mind, xix.—'''2†.''' Ill-sorted; ill-chosen. ''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., ii. 3. 13.

'''unsought''' (un-sât′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unsouht''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''sought.''] Not sought. {{smaller|(''a'') Not searched for; not sought after.}}
{{smaller block|Hopeless to find, yet loath to leave ''unsought.''
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., i. 1. 136.}}
My friends have come to me ''unsought.'' The great God gave them to me.
{{right|''Emerson'', Friendship.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Unasked for; unsolicited.}}
{{smaller block|Love sought is good, but given ''unsought'' is better.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iii. 1. 168.}}}}
